    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Answer choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="She">She</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="He">He</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="his">his</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="her">her</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="studies">studies</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="study">study</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The fictional profile introduces Maya with <span class="ex">she</span>: Maya lives in Chicago. <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="She" data-hint="The second sentence needs the subject form She before works." data-fix="Maya lives in Chicago. She works downtown." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> works downtown. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(subject pronouns)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The fictional profile introduces Luis with <span class="ex">he</span>: Luis loves <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="his" data-hint="The noun job needs the possessive adjective his before it." data-fix="Luis loves his new job." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> new job. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(possessive adjectives)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The weekly study log has Ana as one third-person subject: Ana <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="studies" data-hint="One third-person subject takes studies in the present simple." data-fix="Ana studies English before work." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> English before work. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(present simple)</span></span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice before the rule</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Two coworkers</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">"Do you know Sara? I work with her. She helps me with reports, and I help her with English."</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Who does <span class="ex">her</span> point to?</li>
      <li>Where does <span class="ex">her</span> sit: before or after the verb?</li>
      <li>Find the word that points to the speaker.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You matched each object form to an explicitly introduced person, group, speaker, listener, or thing.">
    <h3>Discovery check: point to the right person or thing</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Think about who or what the pronoun points back to.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Answer choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="me">me</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="him">him</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="her">her</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="them">them</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="us">us</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="it">it</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The fictional profile introduces Omar with <span class="ex">he</span>: This is Omar. Do you know <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="him" data-hint="Omar is the object after know, so he changes to him." data-fix="This is Omar. Do you know him?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The song is one known thing: I love this song. Play <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="it" data-hint="One known thing takes the object form it." data-fix="I love this song. Play it again." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> again.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The two fictional travelers speak together: We’re lost. Can you help <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="us" data-hint="The speakers are we in the first sentence and the object us after help." data-fix="We’re lost. Can you help us?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The message introduces two clients: The clients live far away, so I call <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="them" data-hint="The plural people become the object form them after call." data-fix="The clients live far away, so I call them every week." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> every week.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The fictional speaker requests the explanation: I don’t understand this word. Can you explain it to <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="me" data-hint="After the preposition to, the speaker I changes to the object form me." data-fix="Can you explain it to me?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The profile introduces Ana with <span class="ex">she</span>: Ana is new here, so I eat lunch with <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="her" data-hint="After the preposition with, she changes to the object form her." data-fix="Ana is new here, so I eat lunch with her on Mondays." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> on Mondays.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>The system: subject and object pairs</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Every subject pronoun has a partner form for the object role. Two of them, <span class="ex">you</span> and <span class="ex">it</span>, do not change at all. The word’s position and job, not its appearance alone, tell you the role.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>subject</th><th>object</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>I</td><td><b>me</b></td><td>Call me after the meeting.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>you</td><td><b>you</b></td><td>I can hear you clearly.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>he</td><td><b>him</b></td><td>I work with him.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>she</td><td><b>her</b></td><td>Ask her about the schedule.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>it</td><td><b>it</b></td><td>I need it today.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>we</td><td><b>us</b></td><td>Come with us.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>they</td><td><b>them</b></td><td>Email them the address.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">Use an object pronoun after a verb or a preposition: <span class="ex">see her</span>, <span class="ex">for him</span>, <span class="ex">with them</span>. <span class="ex">They / them</span> can refer to several people or to one person: <span class="ex">A customer left a message. Please call them back.</span></p>

  <h2>Get to the root: roles inside the sentence</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>A pronoun changes form because its <strong>role</strong> changes. The subject is the grammatical starting point, the person or thing connected to the verb before it. An object receives, experiences, or completes the action, or follows a preposition.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>role</th><th>position</th><th>question it answers</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>subject</b></td><td>normally before the verb</td><td>Who does or is this?</td><td><span class="ex">They</span> invited us.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>object of a verb</b></td><td>normally after the verb</td><td>Who or what receives it?</td><td>They invited <span class="ex">us</span>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>object of a preposition</b></td><td>after words such as to, for, with</td><td>To/for/with whom?</td><td>Come with <span class="ex">us</span>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Position: after the verb or after a preposition</h3>
  <p>Object pronouns live in two places: right after the verb, as in <span class="ex">call me</span> and <span class="ex">I know him</span>, or right after a preposition, as in <span class="ex">listen to her</span>, <span class="ex">wait for us</span>, and <span class="ex">work with them</span>. Before the verb, the subject slot still belongs to <span class="ex">I, he, she, we,</span> and <span class="ex">they</span>.</p>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>The classic error</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">I saw her. She saw me.</span></p>
    <p>The same two people swap roles, and the forms swap with them. This is why <span class="ex">I saw she</span> is never possible: after the verb, only the object form works. At A1, keep it simple: before the verb, use <span class="ex">I, he, she</span>; after the verb or a preposition, use <span class="ex">me, him, her</span>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Same spelling, different job: her</h3>
    <p>In <span class="ex">The manager called her</span>, <span class="ex">her</span> stands alone after the verb, so it is an object pronoun. In <span class="ex">She called her manager</span>, <span class="ex">her</span> comes before the noun <span class="ex">manager</span>, so it is a possessive adjective. Ask what job the word does before you name its grammar.</p>
  </div>

  <h3><em>It</em> for a thing, <em>them</em> for plural things</h3>
  <p>Things follow the same system as people. Use <span class="ex">it</span> for one known thing and <span class="ex">them</span> for plural things: <span class="ex">I love this song. Play it again.</span> and <span class="ex">I bought apples. Put them in the fridge.</span> The listener recovers the thing from the sentence before. For people, use the pronoun introduced in the context: <span class="ex">he → him</span>, <span class="ex">she → her</span>, and singular <span class="ex">they → them</span>. Do not guess a pronoun from a person’s name or appearance.</p>

  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Different person or the same person?</h3>
    <p>An ordinary object pronoun normally points to someone different from the subject: <span class="ex">Maya called her</span> means Maya called another person. When the subject and object are the same person, English uses a reflexive form: <span class="ex">Maya called herself</span>. Reflexive pronouns receive a full lesson later; for now, notice that <span class="ex">her</span> and <span class="ex">herself</span> do not mean the same thing.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Sound natural: link the pronoun unless it carries a contrast</h3>
    <p>Object pronouns are often known information, so neutral speech keeps them light and links them to the verb or preposition: <span class="ex"><strong>CALL</strong>-me</span>, <span class="ex"><strong>ASK</strong>-her</span>, <span class="ex"><strong>TELL</strong>-them</span>, and <span class="ex">with-us</span>. Do not insert a pause between the action and its object.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>neutral message</th><th>natural focus</th><th>contrastive message</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Call me after lunch.</td><td><b>CALL</b> me after <b>LUNCH</b>.</td><td>Call <b>ME</b>, not Sara.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>I emailed him yesterday.</td><td>I <b>EMAILED</b> him yesterday.</td><td>I emailed <b>HIM</b>, not her.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Come with us.</td><td><b>COME</b> with us.</td><td>Come with <b>US</b>, not them.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>In fast American speech, an unstressed <span class="ex">him</span> or <span class="ex">her</span> can lose a clear <span class="ex">h</span>, and <span class="ex">them</span> can reduce. You may hear <span class="ex"><strong>CALL</strong>-im</span>, <span class="ex"><strong>ASK</strong>-er</span>, and <span class="ex"><strong>TELL</strong>-em</span>. These are listening approximations, not new spellings. At A1, say the full word clearly and link it smoothly. Stress the pronoun only when the identity is the contrast.</p>
  </div>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read object-pronoun role, reference, and linking check">
    <h2>Listen: what role or reference did you hear?</h2>
    <div class="lead-rule"></div>
    <p>Your teacher reads each fictional line once at a natural speed and once more slowly. Listen through the linked form to identify the pronoun’s job or reference. Open the script only after checking.</p>
    <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You heard subject and object roles, singular them, same versus another thing, and natural pronoun placement.">
      <h3>Tutor-read listening check</h3>
      <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Object-pronoun listening roles and references">
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="subject she">subject she</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="object her">object her</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="one customer">one customer</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="the same report">the same report</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="another report">another report</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="it to him">it to him</button>
      </div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Clip 1 starts the second clause with <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="subject she" data-hint="Listen for the form before can, where the second clause needs a subject." data-fix="Maya says she can help: subject she." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Clip 2 places <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="object her" data-hint="Listen for the light pronoun immediately after the verb call." data-fix="Please call her after lunch: object her." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button> after the verb.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Clip 3 uses <span class="ex">them</span> for <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="one customer" data-hint="The first sentence introduces one customer without specifying he or she." data-fix="Please return it to them: them refers to one customer." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Clip 4 uses <span class="ex">it</span> for <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="the same report" data-hint="The speaker reads one report and then sends that identical document." data-fix="I read the report and sent it to Omar: it means the same report." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Clip 5 uses <span class="ex">one</span> for <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="another report" data-hint="The speaker needs a report cover and asks whether a different example is available." data-fix="Do you have one: one means another report cover of that type." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>Clip 6 orders the two pronouns as <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="it to him" data-hint="The thing pronoun follows send, then to introduces the person pronoun." data-fix="Send it to him today: it to him." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
      <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
    </div>
    <details class="thinking-card">
      <summary>Open the teacher script after checking</summary>
      <ol>
        <li><span class="ex">Maya says she can help.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">Please call her after lunch.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">A customer left this umbrella. Please return it to them.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">I read the report and sent it to Omar.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">I need a clean report cover. Do you have one?</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">Omar needs the file. Send it to him today.</span></li>
      </ol>
      <p>Keep the object pronouns light and linked on the first reading. On the second reading, preserve the same rhythm while making every full written form recoverable.</p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You matched each explicit subject or noun reference to its object form after a verb or preposition.">
    <h3>Drill 1: replace the person or thing</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Find who or what the pronoun points back to. Then choose the object form.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Answer choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="me">me</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="you">you</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="him">him</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="her">her</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="it">it</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="us">us</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="them">them</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The staff profile introduces Mr. Adler with <span class="ex">he</span>: He is busy now. Call <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="him" data-hint="After the verb call, the stated subject form he changes to the object form him." data-fix="Mr. Adler is busy now. Call him after lunch." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> after lunch.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The fictional profile introduces Nia with <span class="ex">she</span>: Nia works downtown, so I have lunch with <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="her" data-hint="After the preposition with, the stated form she changes to her." data-fix="Nia works downtown, so I have lunch with her on Fridays." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> on Fridays.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>One known invoice is ready. I’ll send <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="it" data-hint="One known thing takes it after the verb send." data-fix="The invoice is ready. I’ll send it to the client today." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> to the client today.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The fictional neighbors are two people. We invite <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="them" data-hint="Several people take the object form them after invite." data-fix="Our fictional neighbors are kind. We invite them for dinner sometimes." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> for dinner sometimes.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Two fictional speakers say, “We’re near the station.” You can meet <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="us" data-hint="The speakers are we as subjects and us as the object after meet." data-fix="We’re near the station. You can meet us at the main entrance." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> at the main entrance.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The fictional speaker says, “I’m in the lobby downstairs.” Can you see <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="me" data-hint="The speaker I becomes the object me after see." data-fix="I’m in the lobby downstairs. Can you see me?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">7</span><span>The message is addressed directly to one listener: You’re next on the list. The nurse will call <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="you" data-hint="The form you stays the same in subject and object roles." data-fix="You’re next on the list. The nurse will call you soon." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> soon.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">8</span><span>The story card introduces Luis with <span class="ex">he</span>: Luis tells great stories. I love listening to <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="him" data-hint="After the preposition to, the stated form he changes to him." data-fix="Luis tells great stories. I love listening to him." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Object pronouns with common verb patterns</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Many everyday verbs take an object pronoun right after them, and some take two objects: a person and a thing.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>pattern</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>verb + object</td><td>Call <span class="ex">me</span>. I know <span class="ex">him</span>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>verb + object + thing</td><td>Send <span class="ex">her</span> the link. Tell <span class="ex">us</span> the time.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>verb + thing + to + object</td><td>Send the link to <span class="ex">her</span>. Give it to <span class="ex">them</span>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>preposition + object</td><td>Wait for <span class="ex">me</span>. Sit next to <span class="ex">him</span>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">When the thing is itself a pronoun, neutral U.S. English normally uses <span class="ex">verb + it/them + to + person</span>: <span class="ex">Send it to her</span> and <span class="ex">Give them to us</span>. With a full thing noun, both common patterns work: <span class="ex">Send her the link</span> or <span class="ex">Send the link to her</span>. Avoid the awkward learner pattern <span class="ex">Send her it</span>.</p>

  <h3>Pronouns are reference tools</h3>
  <p>A pronoun lets the listener recover a person or thing without repeating the full noun. The reference must be clear. <span class="ex">I spoke to Maya and sent her the file</span> is clear because <span class="ex">her</span> points back to Maya. If several women were mentioned, repeating the name may be clearer than using <span class="ex">her</span>.</p>
  <p>Person, number, and the introduced pronoun must match the reference: <span class="ex">he → him</span>; <span class="ex">she → her</span>; singular <span class="ex">they → them</span>; one known thing or animal when no personal pronoun is introduced → <span class="ex">it</span>; multiple people or things → <span class="ex">them</span>.</p>
  <div class="note">
    <h3>It, them, and one</h3>
    <p>Use <span class="ex">it/them</span> when referring to the same known thing or things: <span class="ex">Where is my key? I need it.</span> Use <span class="ex">one/ones</span> when you mean another example of that type: <span class="ex">I need a key. Do you have one?</span></p>
  </div>
  <p class="note-line">In the target standard, say <span class="ex">Ana and I are ready</span> because the whole phrase is the subject, but <span class="ex">They invited Ana and me</span> and <span class="ex">between you and me</span> because those pronouns are objects.</p>

  <div class="practice" data-answer-drill data-success-message="Correct. You changed each explicitly stated subject or noun phrase into the matching object form.">
    <h3>Drill 2: type the object pronoun</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Read the cue in parentheses. Type the object pronoun that replaces it.</p>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The profile introduces Sara with <span class="ex">she</span>: I texted <input class="answer-input" data-answer="her" data-hint="After texted, the stated subject form she changes to her." data-fix="I texted her this morning." aria-label="Object pronoun for a person introduced with she after texted" /> this morning. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(she → ?)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The contact card names two clients: I call <input class="answer-input" data-answer="them" data-hint="Two clients take the plural object form them." data-fix="I call them every Sunday evening." aria-label="Object pronoun replacing two clients after call" /> every Sunday evening. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(the two clients)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The profile introduces Tom with <span class="ex">he</span>: Wait for <input class="answer-input" data-answer="him" data-hint="After the preposition for, the stated subject form he changes to him." data-fix="Wait for him outside the office." aria-label="Object pronoun for a person introduced with he after for" /> outside the office. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(he → ?)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>One known report needs a paper copy: Please print <input class="answer-input" data-answer="it" data-hint="One known thing takes the object form it." data-fix="Please print it before the meeting." aria-label="Object pronoun replacing one known report after print" /> before the meeting. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(the report)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The fictional speaker and one coworker form the object group: The manager invited <input class="answer-input" data-answer="us" data-hint="A group that includes the speaker is us in the object role." data-fix="The manager invited us to the workshop." aria-label="Object pronoun for the speaker and one coworker after invited" /> to the workshop. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(my coworker and me)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The fictional speaker asks for help: Can you help <input class="answer-input" data-answer="me" data-hint="The speaker I changes to the object form me after help." data-fix="Can you help me with these bags?" aria-label="Object pronoun for the fictional speaker after help" /> with these staged bags? &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(I → ?)</span></span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
